            <h3>High-Speed vs Regional Trains</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Virginia and the Southeast do not currently have true high-speed rail on this route.</li>
                <li>Amtrak Northeast Regional is a regional/intercity train with consistent speeds up to 125 mph and predictable travel times.</li>
                <li>VRE commuter rail operates at lower speeds with frequent stops, designed for daily commuters rather than long-distance travelers.</li>
                <li>The Carolinian and Silver Service are long-distance trains connecting New York with North Carolina or Florida, with intermediate stops in Alexandria and Fredericksburg.</li>
                <li>While comparable to driving time, the train offers comfortable seating, scenic views, and city-center arrivals, which can save significant time compared to driving during the notorious heavy I-95 rush hour congestion in this corridor.</li>
                <li>Plans for the Southeast High Speed Rail Corridor and Virginia's Long Bridge Project may eventually improve speeds and capacity on this route.</li>
            </ul>
